\DOC GEN_PROVE_SAFETY_SPEC_TAC

\TYPE {GEN_PROVE_SAFETY_SPEC_TAC : term list -> tactic -> thm * (thm option array) -> thm list -> ((thm * (thm option array)) -> string -> tactic) -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Fully discharges an existentially quantified constant-time/safety specification
goal about a machine program, leaving no subgoals.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes five arguments: a {term list} naming the variables treated as
public inputs; a {tactic} for the execution/decoding steps; a
{thm * (thm option array)} pairing a program-length theorem with a
per-instruction table; a {thm list} of extra rewrite theorems used on the
loading hypothesis; and a step-generating function of type {(thm * (thm option
array)) -> string -> tactic}, which is instantiated with the program data to
drive stepping. It applies to goals of the form
{
   A ?- ?f. !x1 ... xn. t ==> u
          ==========================  GEN_PROVE_SAFETY_SPEC_TAC [public inputs] exec_tac (len_th, instr_table) [rewrites] step_fn
}
\noindent where {u} is an {ensures} specification whose postcondition
constrains the event trace produced relative to the initial one and asserts
memory-access bounds, and {t} is a conjunction of side conditions on the
quantified addresses. The goal is proved outright, with nothing of {A} left
behind.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces it is used on such existentially quantified,
implication-shaped {ensures} goals with no assumptions and the same shape of
argument package, so only this single closing behaviour is attested.

\ENDDOC
